WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:00.633
Hi everyone

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.600
I'm Wei Zhichao

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.333
The book I'm unpacking for you today

00:00:03.333 --> 00:00:06.233
is called The Good Psychopath's
Guide to Success

00:00:06.666 --> 00:00:09.100
This book has one fascinating insight

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.800
It says that every single one of us

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.833
is born a psychopath

00:00:13.066 --> 00:00:16.100
a bit like those cold-blooded
killers in the movies

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.433
like Hannibal

00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:19.866
like Dexter from the TV show Dexter

00:00:19.866 --> 00:00:22.633
like the serial killers in Mindhunter

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.600
You were once just like them too

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:25.933
calm, decisive

00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:26.833
fearless

00:00:26.833 --> 00:00:28.000
brimming with charm

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.600
not caring what anyone thought of you

00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:31.733
That was back when you were a baby

00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:33.500
or even still a fetus

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.833
That's really what you were like

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.333
Then society spent decades

00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:40.800
taking the factory-default settings
you were born with

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.500
and slowly editing them away

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.233
Socialization installed fear in you

00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:46.500
installed the urge to please

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.733
installed the habit of reading
other people's faces

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.233
Your hesitation now

00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:51.700
the second-guessing, the anxiety

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.300
isn't who you were born to be

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.000
it's that you were domesticated

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.233
In other words

00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:59.066
the reason you always fall just short
at the crucial moment

00:00:59.066 --> 00:01:00.400
not striking when you should

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.800
lacking the nerve to get done
what needs doing

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.800
very likely isn't that you're not smart enough

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:05.800
or not working hard enough

00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:06.600
it's because

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.433
there's too little psychopath left in you

00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:11.066
You're too normal

00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:12.400
This book

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:13.500
is here to help you see clearly

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.700
exactly what society edited out of you

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.066
and whether you need to take some of it

00:01:18.066 --> 00:01:19.033
back

00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:21.733
to become a little more like a psychopath again

00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:23.300
the good kind, of course, as in the title

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:24.266
the good psychopath

00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:26.400
The two authors who wrote this book

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.233
are a truly remarkable pairing

00:01:28.466 --> 00:01:29.866
One is Kevin Dutton

00:01:29.866 --> 00:01:31.800
a psychologist at Oxford

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.833
a world-leading authority
on psychopaths

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.266
And the other author

00:01:36.266 --> 00:01:38.266
is almost too legendary to be real

00:01:38.466 --> 00:01:40.500
His name is Andy McNab

00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:42.900
but that name is actually a pseudonym

00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:45.266
His real name and face
are secret to this day

00:01:45.433 --> 00:01:47.700
He's still a target with a bounty

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.400
put on him by several terrorist groups

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.533
When he appears in the media

00:01:50.533 --> 00:01:52.866
he's either blurred out
or sitting in shadow

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.133
Because McNab

00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:56.466
was once an ace special forces operator

00:01:56.466 --> 00:01:59.033
in the British SAS

00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:02.100
He carried out countless
high-risk military missions

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.033
For instance, during the Gulf War

00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:05.033
he slipped behind enemy lines

00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:07.066
to hunt down Scud launchers

00:02:07.066 --> 00:02:09.400
to sever Iraq's communications lifeline

00:02:09.666 --> 00:02:10.666
The day he retired

00:02:10.666 --> 00:02:13.700
McNab was the most highly decorated

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.266
serving soldier in the entire British Army

00:02:15.666 --> 00:02:16.733
And after leaving the service

00:02:16.733 --> 00:02:19.466
he reinvented himself
as a bestselling author

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.633
He also writes scripts, produces films

00:02:21.633 --> 00:02:24.433
and invests in startups
in Britain and America

00:02:24.866 --> 00:02:27.533
Yet this top-tier specimen of a man

00:02:27.533 --> 00:02:30.066
is, without a doubt, a psychopath

00:02:30.533 --> 00:02:31.800
As the book opens

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:32.833
the first author, Dutton

00:02:32.833 --> 00:02:35.100
brings this legend into his lab

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.500
and shows him beheadings

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:37.233
torture

00:02:37.233 --> 00:02:39.800
severed limbs, the goriest images there are

00:02:40.233 --> 00:02:42.900
And McNab's heart rate and stress levels

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.400
didn't rise, they actually fell

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.600
dropping below his baseline

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:48.233
as calm as a dead man

00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:51.900
Dutton's verdict was: you're a psychopath

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.400
the very top tier of them

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.866
but you're a good psychopath

00:02:56.633 --> 00:02:58.900
So what exactly is a good psychopath

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.200
and how were you domesticated by society

00:03:01.533 --> 00:03:02.200
What, specifically, should you

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.866
take back today

00:03:03.866 --> 00:03:06.400
to summon those lost traits home

00:03:06.433 --> 00:03:09.433
so you stop churning, fretting, hesitating

00:03:09.433 --> 00:03:12.100
and become as free as a good psychopath?

00:03:12.333 --> 00:03:13.733
Let's take it one piece at a time

00:03:14.333 --> 00:03:17.266
First we need to nail down
what psychopathy means

00:03:17.666 --> 00:03:19.600
When you hear the word psychopath

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:20.733
the image that pops into your head

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:23.700
is probably Hannibal eating a brain

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.133
or some serial killer
from a crime documentary

00:03:26.133 --> 00:03:29.033
still smiling as they're arrested

00:03:29.333 --> 00:03:31.433
Hollywood spent decades

00:03:31.433 --> 00:03:33.733
building that reflex in you

00:03:34.033 --> 00:03:35.533
But to a psychologist

00:03:35.533 --> 00:03:37.700
what the word psychopathy points to

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.500
is nothing like that impression

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:40.700
In psychology

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:41.833
psychopathy actually refers

00:03:41.833 --> 00:03:42.300
to

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.333
a very specific cluster
of personality traits

00:03:45.666 --> 00:03:48.433
Dutton, the first author,
lists them in the book

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:49.533
These traits

00:03:49.533 --> 00:03:50.733
include fearlessness

00:03:50.733 --> 00:03:52.100
being afraid of nothing

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.533
decisiveness, calm, confidence, focus

00:03:55.533 --> 00:03:57.733
charm, high stress tolerance, low empathy

00:03:57.733 --> 00:03:59.333
Notice

00:03:59.333 --> 00:04:02.133
there's no item here called liking to kill

00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:04.733
and none called
enjoying the torture of others

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:08.266
The traits I just listed
aren't evil in themselves

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.666
They show up in serial killers

00:04:10.666 --> 00:04:11.700
but they also show up

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.200
in some of the top elites in their fields

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.266
like top surgeons

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.900
In the book, Dutton interviews
a brain surgeon

00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:21.233
who says

00:04:21.233 --> 00:04:22.466
to me, surgery

00:04:22.466 --> 00:04:24.633
is a bloody competitive sport

00:04:24.833 --> 00:04:27.300
You can't let your own fear paralyze you

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.433
You can't go soft

00:04:28.433 --> 00:04:30.066
just because you're about to

00:04:30.066 --> 00:04:33.200
cut open someone's skull

00:04:33.233 --> 00:04:34.533
you have to be 100% focused

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.800
You have to be merciless

00:04:37.266 --> 00:04:37.666
with absolute confidence in your judgment

00:04:37.666 --> 00:04:39.733
See

00:04:39.733 --> 00:04:40.900
if you didn't know the source

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.266
you could easily assume this

00:04:43.533 --> 00:04:44.433
was a mob boss's creed

00:04:44.433 --> 00:04:46.666
But it comes from

00:04:47.433 --> 00:04:47.933
a surgeon who saves lives every day

00:04:47.933 --> 00:04:50.466
Likewise

00:04:50.466 --> 00:04:51.300
these traits hold up
in a hedge fund manager

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:54.400
as well

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:56.066
Dutton interviewed a top fund manager

00:04:56.066 --> 00:04:58.066
who said: when others panic

00:04:58.066 --> 00:04:59.533
that's exactly when I'm calmest

00:04:59.533 --> 00:05:01.333
It was that calm

00:05:01.333 --> 00:05:04.000
that in 2008, when the market
fell twenty

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:06.933
or thirty percent, let me gain 20% instead

00:05:07.233 --> 00:05:08.700
These traits hold up in lawyers too

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.200
The very best trial lawyers

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.000
all have a near-narcissistic
flair for performance

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.900
and that unapologetic confidence

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:17.933
The statistics back up these impressions too

00:05:17.933 --> 00:05:20.400
In the fields I just mentioned

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.633
the share of psychopaths
among the top performers

00:05:22.233 --> 00:05:24.900
is absurdly high

00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:25.600
Dutton offers a great metaphor in the book

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.466
He says

00:05:28.466 --> 00:05:29.866
you can take these traits a psychopath has

00:05:29.866 --> 00:05:32.000
ruthlessness, fearlessness

00:05:32.033 --> 00:05:33.400
confidence, mental toughness

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:36.100
and picture each trait

00:05:36.266 --> 00:05:38.333
as a dial on a mixing desk

00:05:38.333 --> 00:05:40.266
the kind a DJ uses to mix music

00:05:40.266 --> 00:05:41.666
Every dial can be turned up

00:05:42.233 --> 00:05:43.900
or turned down

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.433
Those psychopathic killers

00:05:45.666 --> 00:05:47.500
where does their problem lie?

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:48.466
Their problem is that these dials

00:05:48.466 --> 00:05:51.233
are all jammed

00:05:51.466 --> 00:05:53.833
stuck, and stuck at maximum

00:05:53.833 --> 00:05:56.033
Turn ruthlessness all the way up and jam it

00:05:56.033 --> 00:05:58.133
and you're infinitely ruthless to everyone

00:05:58.133 --> 00:06:00.033
killing won't faze you at all

00:06:00.033 --> 00:06:02.433
But what if you can,
depending on the situation

00:06:02.666 --> 00:06:04.666
flexibly adjust these dials?

00:06:04.666 --> 00:06:06.433
Then you can: be calm when you need calm

00:06:06.433 --> 00:06:07.900
show empathy when you need empathy

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.033
be decisive when you need decisiveness

00:06:10.033 --> 00:06:11.533
Then you'll be like the surgeons I mentioned

00:06:11.666 --> 00:06:14.100
like those trading masters

00:06:14.666 --> 00:06:15.500
Then you're a good psychopath

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:16.633
Dutton says

00:06:16.633 --> 00:06:18.866
the difference between a good psychopath

00:06:18.866 --> 00:06:20.233
and a bad one

00:06:20.633 --> 00:06:23.233
comes down to just three things

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:25.833
First, they cause no unnecessary harm

00:06:25.833 --> 00:06:27.266
Second, they're mentally flexible enough

00:06:27.266 --> 00:06:28.866
to adjust their behavior

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:29.733
to the specific situation

00:06:29.733 --> 00:06:32.100
Third

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:33.600
the way they use these psychopathic traits

00:06:34.033 --> 00:06:34.833
benefits society

00:06:34.833 --> 00:06:37.466
So you see

00:06:37.466 --> 00:06:39.600
is psychopathy a gift or a curse?

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:40.700
It depends entirely on how you use it

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:41.733
The mixing desk is

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.666
the same mixing desk

00:06:43.666 --> 00:06:44.900
but what it produces can be a symphony

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:46.866
or it can be noise

00:06:46.866 --> 00:06:49.200
In that Gulf War operation

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.400
behind enemy lines that I mentioned earlier

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.500
the second author, McNab

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:54.900
led a small SAS squad

00:06:55.033 --> 00:06:56.500
deep behind Iraqi lines on a mission

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:58.533
And their hideout

00:06:58.866 --> 00:07:00.433
was discovered by a boy herding sheep

00:07:00.666 --> 00:07:02.600
Just how urgent was this problem?

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.833
A few hundred meters from their hideout

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:06.333
was an Iraqi anti-aircraft gun position

00:07:06.333 --> 00:07:08.800
If they let the boy go

00:07:08.866 --> 00:07:10.300
he'd soon carry word to those gunners

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.633
and then it would all be over

00:07:12.933 --> 00:07:15.833
But what about killing the boy?

00:07:15.933 --> 00:07:18.033
Killing him could indeed buy
some time before exposure

00:07:18.033 --> 00:07:20.133
but what if they were caught anyway

00:07:20.133 --> 00:07:21.900
Getting caught with a child's body

00:07:22.333 --> 00:07:24.833
how do you think they'd treat you?

00:07:24.833 --> 00:07:26.300
So McNab had to make a decision

00:07:26.633 --> 00:07:29.033
and make it very fast

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.900
He immediately chose to let the boy go

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:34.933
He later said: "We're the British SAS

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.033
we are not the SS"

00:07:37.066 --> 00:07:38.866
But here's the interesting part

00:07:38.933 --> 00:07:41.233
the first author, Dutton, argues

00:07:41.233 --> 00:07:43.700
this seemingly moral decision

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:45.400
was actually, at its core,
a psychopathic

00:07:45.733 --> 00:07:46.900
and utterly cold-blooded calculation

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:50.033
What McNab really did

00:07:50.333 --> 00:07:52.466
was precisely turn the dials in his brain

00:07:52.466 --> 00:07:54.133
Some dials he turned up

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.933
like the fearlessness dial

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:58.466
He knew letting the boy go
might get him killed

00:07:58.833 --> 00:08:00.133
but he accepted that risk

00:08:00.133 --> 00:08:01.333
There's also the confidence dial

00:08:01.333 --> 00:08:02.633
trusting that whatever happened

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:04.200
he could handle it

00:08:04.233 --> 00:08:06.333
And the focus dial

00:08:06.333 --> 00:08:06.933
He stayed fixed on the real objective

00:08:06.933 --> 00:08:09.233
which was to find

00:08:09.233 --> 00:08:11.333
and destroy valuable strategic targets

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:12.700
not to be thrown off
by the crisis in front of him

00:08:12.733 --> 00:08:14.400
And calm

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:15.900
His mind was spinning at high speed

00:08:16.133 --> 00:08:16.833
while his exterior didn't move a muscle

00:08:16.833 --> 00:08:18.100
And some dials

00:08:18.133 --> 00:08:19.233
he turned down

00:08:19.233 --> 00:08:21.633
like impulsiveness

00:08:21.633 --> 00:08:22.866
he didn't whip out his gun on instinct

00:08:22.866 --> 00:08:24.666
and ruthlessness, of course

00:08:24.666 --> 00:08:26.233
he didn't choose the most efficient

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:27.600
but most brutal option

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:28.533
McNab said

00:08:28.533 --> 00:08:30.000
"In a situation like that

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.466
you have to see the whole picture

00:08:31.666 --> 00:08:32.866
not just what's in front of you

00:08:32.866 --> 00:08:34.266
You have to think like a chess game

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:35.800
several moves ahead

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:36.933
Killing that kid right off the bat

00:08:37.133 --> 00:08:39.266
that's panicking

00:08:39.266 --> 00:08:41.233
We in the SAS don't take panickers

00:08:41.866 --> 00:08:42.666
because panic only backfires"

00:08:42.666 --> 00:08:44.066
So you see

00:08:44.066 --> 00:08:46.933
letting that shepherd boy go

00:08:47.066 --> 00:08:47.833
this decision looks like a moral choice

00:08:47.833 --> 00:08:50.700
but at its core

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.666
it was a tactical judgment
calm to the extreme

00:08:52.666 --> 00:08:53.900
Kill him and you'd have
to carry the body

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:54.800
it becomes a liability

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.866
and if caught

00:08:57.233 --> 00:08:58.866
that dead child can't be explained away

00:08:58.866 --> 00:09:00.200
Not killing him risked exposure

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:01.333
but at least once caught

00:09:01.333 --> 00:09:02.466
you're still a regular soldier

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:05.100
not a butcher

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:06.200
Every step was a precise weighing
of pros and cons

00:09:06.666 --> 00:09:07.833
not impulse

00:09:07.833 --> 00:09:10.333
This is how a good

00:09:10.333 --> 00:09:11.666
psychopath's brain operates

00:09:11.666 --> 00:09:13.666
They can turn every dial

00:09:14.133 --> 00:09:15.500
to exactly the right setting

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:17.500
Of course, the case I just told

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:19.033
if it made you feel a certain unease

00:09:19.033 --> 00:09:20.033
if you caught a whiff of danger

00:09:20.033 --> 00:09:21.333
then you're right

00:09:21.866 --> 00:09:23.800
I'll come back to it at the end

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:25.100
For now, let's look at the next question

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:26.833
People like McNab

00:09:26.833 --> 00:09:28.733
and those elite surgeons

00:09:28.866 --> 00:09:31.433
twisted killers, ace lawyers

00:09:31.433 --> 00:09:33.233
this whole psychopathic
personality setup of theirs

00:09:33.833 --> 00:09:34.333
where did it come from in the first place?

00:09:34.333 --> 00:09:37.233
Well

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:39.233
this setup is actually
something everyone is born with

00:09:39.233 --> 00:09:41.600
Every one of us humans

00:09:42.066 --> 00:09:42.533
already had it before we were even born

00:09:42.533 --> 00:09:43.633
Of course

00:09:43.633 --> 00:09:46.500
I should clarify

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:47.866
this conclusion is less a scientific claim

00:09:47.866 --> 00:09:50.533
and more a brilliant insight

00:09:50.866 --> 00:09:53.007
the two authors arrived at

00:09:53.007 --> 00:09:53.666
They start from the work of
a Harvard biologist

00:09:53.666 --> 00:09:54.866
David Haig

00:09:54.866 --> 00:09:57.500
It begins with his research

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:58.600
David Haig spent several years

00:09:58.733 --> 00:10:01.833
studying one thing

00:10:01.833 --> 00:10:03.733
the relationship between a mother
and the fetus in her womb

00:10:03.733 --> 00:10:05.400
Is it really, as most people imagine

00:10:05.866 --> 00:10:07.700
full of sweetness and warmth?

00:10:08.033 --> 00:10:09.600
His conclusion: not at all

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:12.500
What the fetus does to the mother

00:10:12.933 --> 00:10:14.266
is more like a carefully planned robbery

00:10:14.266 --> 00:10:16.833
While you're still in the womb

00:10:16.866 --> 00:10:19.466
you've already started
working your mother over

00:10:19.466 --> 00:10:21.300
Move one: your placenta injects
into the mother's blood

00:10:21.333 --> 00:10:23.533
a key protein

00:10:23.533 --> 00:10:25.733
It forces the mother's body to send more blood

00:10:26.133 --> 00:10:28.433
and more nutrients into the placenta

00:10:28.433 --> 00:10:30.233
This is no gentle request

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:32.933
it's a biological hijacking

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:35.333
It dangerously drives up
the mother's blood pressure

00:10:35.933 --> 00:10:37.266
This is the source of
gestational hypertension

00:10:37.533 --> 00:10:38.466
Move two is even harsher

00:10:38.466 --> 00:10:41.400
Your fetal cells

00:10:41.433 --> 00:10:43.800
release something called placental lactogen

00:10:44.266 --> 00:10:45.600
specifically designed to counter
the mother's insulin

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.833
What does insulin do?

00:10:47.866 --> 00:10:49.733
It's in charge of lowering blood sugar

00:10:50.033 --> 00:10:52.400
and storing that energy away

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:54.466
And the placental lactogen the fetus releases

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:55.500
is essentially telling
your mother's body

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:57.266
don't store it

00:10:57.633 --> 00:10:59.000
keep all the blood sugar in the blood

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:00.833
Then you, through the placenta

00:11:00.833 --> 00:11:01.800
intercept that high-concentration
blood sugar

00:11:02.266 --> 00:11:04.133
and take it for yourself

00:11:04.333 --> 00:11:06.466
Just how extreme does this get?

00:11:06.466 --> 00:11:07.866
There's a very serious
pregnancy complication

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:09.133
called preeclampsia

00:11:09.266 --> 00:11:11.433
and this is what causes it

00:11:11.833 --> 00:11:14.300
It can put the mother's life in danger

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:16.833
This is literally an unborn child

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:20.500
waging chemical warfare on its own mother

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:22.400
After hearing Dutton lay out
this research, McNab said

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:23.233
"Forget Gaddafi and Saddam

00:11:23.233 --> 00:11:25.666
when it comes to chemical warfare

00:11:26.133 --> 00:11:27.733
the real champion is the unborn child"

00:11:27.733 --> 00:11:28.900
And what's even better

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:30.733
is that once out of the womb

00:11:31.066 --> 00:11:33.322
this whole operation doesn't actually stop

00:11:33.322 --> 00:11:35.033
Think about it: what's a baby's image?

00:11:35.033 --> 00:11:36.633
They're adorable and charming

00:11:36.866 --> 00:11:37.733
you can't help wanting to hold them

00:11:37.733 --> 00:11:38.833
But think carefully

00:11:38.933 --> 00:11:41.066
about what they're doing

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:42.500
They are completely self-centered

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:44.200
They don't care if you're tired

00:11:44.233 --> 00:11:46.866
sleepy, or falling apart

00:11:47.133 --> 00:11:48.800
They only care
whether their needs are met

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:50.100
They use crying to control you

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:51.233
smiles to win you over

00:11:51.233 --> 00:11:53.700
and those big eyes

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:56.300
staring at you, unblinking, for ages

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:59.133
until you're willing
to give them everything

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:00.133
And this long, unblinking stare

00:12:00.133 --> 00:12:03.233
in psychology

00:12:03.333 --> 00:12:06.133
is a hallmark of
social disinhibition

00:12:06.133 --> 00:12:08.633
a classic behavioral trait of psychopaths

00:12:08.633 --> 00:12:10.300
When ordinary adults
stare at someone too long

00:12:10.333 --> 00:12:13.233
they get embarrassed first

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:14.800
But psychopaths can do it
without any discomfort

00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:16.200
Dutton puts it this way

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:17.100
a baby's charm

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:18.200
knack for manipulation

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.000
pure self-interest

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:21.133
this is exactly the same
behavioral pattern

00:12:21.633 --> 00:12:22.833
as a psychopath's

00:12:22.833 --> 00:12:25.266
So psychopathy

00:12:25.266 --> 00:12:26.666
is in some sense our natural state

00:12:27.066 --> 00:12:28.800
We're born this way

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:30.466
The moment you came into this world

00:12:30.466 --> 00:12:32.666
evolution had already equipped you

00:12:32.733 --> 00:12:35.266
with a full psychopath toolkit

00:12:35.266 --> 00:12:36.833
It lets you seize resources
most efficiently

00:12:36.833 --> 00:12:38.600
and lets you, in this dangerous world

00:12:39.233 --> 00:12:40.033
survive with the best possible odds

00:12:40.033 --> 00:12:41.500
But the problem is

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:44.000
as you grow up day by day

00:12:44.233 --> 00:12:46.133
these tools get confiscated one by one

00:12:46.133 --> 00:12:47.266
Society teaches you
not to be so selfish

00:12:47.266 --> 00:12:48.933
It loves you

00:12:48.933 --> 00:12:50.500
and punishes you when you do wrong

00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:51.633
It instills all kinds of morals

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.700
all kinds of norms

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:57.266
Society spent decades

00:12:57.266 --> 00:13:00.200
taking the boldness, fearlessness, and calm
you were born with

00:13:00.433 --> 00:13:02.266
and slowly, systematically
removing it from you

00:13:02.266 --> 00:13:03.933
This socialization is, of course, vital

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:06.000
without it there'd be no civilization

00:13:06.066 --> 00:13:07.600
But it's also because of it

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:09.900
that by the time you finally grow up

00:13:10.066 --> 00:13:11.433
and finally have the power
to decide for yourself

00:13:11.433 --> 00:13:12.433
you often find you've already

00:13:12.633 --> 00:13:13.866
been frightened

00:13:13.866 --> 00:13:15.066
frightened by choice

00:13:15.066 --> 00:13:16.666
frightened by consequences

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:17.933
frightened by freedom itself

00:13:17.933 --> 00:13:20.066
Socialization

00:13:20.066 --> 00:13:21.300
installed lock after lock in your brain

00:13:21.666 --> 00:13:22.666
sealing you up

00:13:22.666 --> 00:13:24.700
After finishing this book

00:13:24.833 --> 00:13:27.633
I think the deadliest are three locks

00:13:28.066 --> 00:13:30.666
fear, reputation, and empathy

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:32.300
Let's look at what each of these locks is

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:33.900
and how a good psychopath

00:13:34.633 --> 00:13:36.066
goes about unlocking them

00:13:36.066 --> 00:13:38.666
The first lock, fear

00:13:39.066 --> 00:13:40.833
naturally locks up the fearlessness dial

00:13:40.833 --> 00:13:41.800
Ever notice that we adults

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:44.000
are actually, a lot of the time

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:47.100
more timid than children?

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:49.900
Part of this comes from socialization

00:13:49.933 --> 00:13:51.200
A lot of the so-called wisdom
society teaches us

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:52.900
look before you leap

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:54.233
wait for the right moment

00:13:54.333 --> 00:13:56.733
impulse is the devil

00:13:56.733 --> 00:13:58.200
all this advice that makes you seem mature

00:13:58.200 --> 00:13:59.333
seem steady

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:02.100
what is it at its core?

00:14:02.533 --> 00:14:05.266
At its core it's telling you
to be afraid of something

00:14:05.266 --> 00:14:06.533
to know there are consequences
that will scare you

00:14:07.033 --> 00:14:09.133
so take it easy

00:14:09.133 --> 00:14:10.733
So a lot of the time you can't move

00:14:10.733 --> 00:14:11.866
you don't dare anything out of line

00:14:12.233 --> 00:14:13.300
anything extraordinary

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:14.933
Kids dare anything

00:14:15.333 --> 00:14:17.133
adults look before and after

00:14:17.133 --> 00:14:18.933
But this fear in adults

00:14:19.033 --> 00:14:20.633
isn't only socialization

00:14:21.266 --> 00:14:23.866
there's something deeper here

00:14:23.866 --> 00:14:25.866
The 19th-century Danish philosopher
Kierkegaard

00:14:26.233 --> 00:14:27.633
offered a wonderful metaphor

00:14:27.633 --> 00:14:29.233
He said: imagine

00:14:29.233 --> 00:14:31.600
you're standing on the edge of a cliff

00:14:31.933 --> 00:14:34.333
you'd actually feel two kinds of fear

00:14:34.433 --> 00:14:35.333
The first is the fear of falling off

00:14:35.333 --> 00:14:36.266
that's normal

00:14:36.633 --> 00:14:39.233
easy to understand

00:14:39.466 --> 00:14:42.600
But the second fear is the truly deadly one

00:14:43.066 --> 00:14:44.633
you're afraid you'll jump
of your own accord

00:14:44.633 --> 00:14:46.400
You could clearly choose not to jump

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:49.100
but you suddenly realize

00:14:49.733 --> 00:14:51.200
whether you jump is entirely up to you

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:51.866
And this "entirely up to you"

00:14:51.866 --> 00:14:54.833
itself

00:14:55.233 --> 00:14:56.533
gives you a scalp-tingling vertigo

00:14:56.533 --> 00:14:58.533
Kierkegaard said

00:14:58.533 --> 00:15:02.466
anxiety is the dizziness of freedom

00:15:02.933 --> 00:15:03.600
Fear, it turns out, comes from freedom

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:05.833
Think about it

00:15:06.266 --> 00:15:08.066
isn't this kind of dizziness everywhere?

00:15:08.066 --> 00:15:09.100
You could absolutely quit your job

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:10.200
no one's stopping you

00:15:10.633 --> 00:15:12.666
but you just don't dare

00:15:12.733 --> 00:15:14.200
You could absolutely confess your feelings

00:15:14.533 --> 00:15:15.933
but you just can't get the words out

00:15:15.933 --> 00:15:17.300
Start a business, switch careers

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:18.066
move to a new city

00:15:18.066 --> 00:15:20.600
A lot of the time

00:15:20.933 --> 00:15:21.633
there's no outside force stopping you

00:15:21.633 --> 00:15:24.066
What stops you

00:15:24.533 --> 00:15:26.466
is precisely that you have complete freedom

00:15:26.466 --> 00:15:28.133
You stand at the cliff edge of life

00:15:28.133 --> 00:15:30.066
not afraid of falling

00:15:30.533 --> 00:15:31.933
but afraid you'll jump

00:15:31.933 --> 00:15:34.600
I think this is, in this whole book

00:15:35.033 --> 00:15:35.666
the insight that gives me the most chills

00:15:35.666 --> 00:15:36.233
At first glance

00:15:36.233 --> 00:15:37.666
a lot of the time

00:15:37.666 --> 00:15:39.233
when we don't dare to decide

00:15:39.233 --> 00:15:41.066
what we seem to fear is the unknown

00:15:41.466 --> 00:15:42.900
the uncertainty of the road ahead

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:44.466
But think harder

00:15:44.466 --> 00:15:47.233
and maybe what we truly fear

00:15:47.633 --> 00:15:48.666
is that this is entirely my own choice

00:15:48.666 --> 00:15:50.233
No one's forcing you

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:52.733
no fate is pushing you

00:15:53.033 --> 00:15:54.866
you're the one pushing yourself off

00:15:55.666 --> 00:15:57.900
What you fear is really freedom

00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:00.600
But whether it comes from socialization

00:16:00.666 --> 00:16:01.633
or from this dizziness of freedom

00:16:01.633 --> 00:16:04.266
most ordinary people

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:06.800
have this fear of not daring to decide

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:07.866
So do psychopaths have this hesitation

00:16:08.133 --> 00:16:09.500
this fear?

00:16:09.833 --> 00:16:10.800
They hardly do

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:12.333
McNab says

00:16:12.333 --> 00:16:14.066
a plank three feet wide

00:16:14.066 --> 00:16:15.300
set one meter off the ground

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:16.500
anyone can walk across it

00:16:16.733 --> 00:16:17.400
even with their eyes closed

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:19.600
But likewise

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:20.466
set that plank 3,000 feet in the air

00:16:20.466 --> 00:16:21.900
and you freeze

00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:23.700
you don't dare move at all

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:25.100
He says: why are you looking
at both sides?

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:27.466
Just walk across, that's all

00:16:27.866 --> 00:16:29.433
Man, I envy these psychopaths

00:16:29.433 --> 00:16:32.433
He's drawn another analogy too

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:34.400
He says: why doesn't a plane
fall out of the sky?

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:37.300
Aerodynamics aside

00:16:37.433 --> 00:16:40.200
the most basic reason
is that it's flying too fast

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:41.266
If a plane mid-flight
suddenly became conscious

00:16:41.266 --> 00:16:42.433
and started thinking, wait

00:16:42.433 --> 00:16:43.833
how am I up in the sky

00:16:43.833 --> 00:16:46.433
am I going to fall?

00:16:46.833 --> 00:16:48.333
then every airline in the world
would go bankrupt

00:16:48.333 --> 00:16:49.933
He says: planes don't think

00:16:49.933 --> 00:16:51.100
so planes don't crash

00:16:51.100 --> 00:16:52.466
You think too much

00:16:53.033 --> 00:16:54.733
so you crash

00:16:55.100 --> 00:16:57.333
All right, so how do you
break this deadlock?

00:16:57.333 --> 00:16:58.633
The way psychopaths defeat fear

00:16:58.633 --> 00:17:01.033
isn't through bravery

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:02.300
isn't gritting their teeth
and telling themselves I'm not scared

00:17:02.300 --> 00:17:04.233
The way they crack it

00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:06.233
is a cognitive shift

00:17:06.233 --> 00:17:08.266
Psychologists ran an experiment

00:17:08.266 --> 00:17:09.866
having psychopaths and ordinary people
together

00:17:10.133 --> 00:17:11.133
do a learning task

00:17:11.133 --> 00:17:13.700
In the first condition

00:17:13.833 --> 00:17:14.200
mistakes were punished
with an electric shock

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:16.500
The result:

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:17.266
psychopaths learned even slower
than ordinary people

00:17:17.266 --> 00:17:19.333
In other words

00:17:19.733 --> 00:17:21.433
they're not very sensitive to punishment

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:23.233
But the key is the second condition

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:24.033
a correct answer earned a cash reward

00:17:24.033 --> 00:17:25.433
This time

00:17:25.433 --> 00:17:26.833
the psychopaths' learning speed

00:17:26.833 --> 00:17:28.300
suddenly shot up

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:30.933
far surpassing ordinary people

00:17:30.933 --> 00:17:32.400
So a psychopath's fearlessness

00:17:32.433 --> 00:17:34.900
isn't being afraid of nothing

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:37.300
they just don't care much about punishment

00:17:37.666 --> 00:17:40.433
but they're extremely sensitive to reward

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:42.133
Their attention isn't on
what if I lose

00:17:42.133 --> 00:17:43.900
but locked firmly onto

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:45.066
what I'll get if I win

00:17:45.066 --> 00:17:46.933
This is why

00:17:46.933 --> 00:17:48.533
so many ordinary people and psychopaths

00:17:48.533 --> 00:17:50.300
facing the very same situation

00:17:50.933 --> 00:17:53.666
make completely different decisions

00:17:54.033 --> 00:17:55.533
For example, the book gives
a taxi driver example

00:17:55.533 --> 00:17:57.000
London taxi drivers

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:57.866
when the weather's good

00:17:57.866 --> 00:17:59.066
at those times

00:17:59.066 --> 00:18:00.466
there are more people out hailing cabs

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:02.433
so business is better

00:18:02.666 --> 00:18:03.466
and yet they knock off early

00:18:03.466 --> 00:18:06.033
because that day

00:18:06.466 --> 00:18:07.900
they quickly hit their daily quota

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:09.133
And when the weather's bad

00:18:09.133 --> 00:18:10.633
when fewer people are out

00:18:10.866 --> 00:18:13.000
business is grim

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:14.400
they instead work harder, longer

00:18:14.733 --> 00:18:15.200
trying to make up the loss

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:17.066
They figure

00:18:17.066 --> 00:18:18.133
if they didn't earn the quota that day

00:18:18.533 --> 00:18:20.000
they've lost out

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:21.900
But do you see the problem?

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:22.600
This is completely backwards

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:23.833
When the weather's good

00:18:23.833 --> 00:18:25.400
that's exactly when to work hard

00:18:25.533 --> 00:18:26.433
to maximize profit

00:18:26.433 --> 00:18:27.700
When the weather's bad

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:28.466
that's when you should knock off early

00:18:28.466 --> 00:18:29.866
conserve your energy

00:18:30.233 --> 00:18:32.666
and wait for the next good day

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:34.133
But ordinary people often don't do this

00:18:34.133 --> 00:18:35.900
because ordinary people's attention

00:18:35.933 --> 00:18:38.400
is forever fixed on I can't lose

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:39.900
rather than how can I win more

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:43.033
The two authors say

00:18:43.033 --> 00:18:44.000
those moments in life
when fear places your bets

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:44.800
you don't dare quit

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:45.800
don't dare speak up

00:18:45.833 --> 00:18:46.433
don't dare wager

00:18:46.433 --> 00:18:49.466
essentially

00:18:49.633 --> 00:18:51.100
you're making the same mistake
as these taxi drivers

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:53.666
You put all your attention

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:55.133
on what if I fail

00:18:55.133 --> 00:18:56.500
While psychopaths

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:57.733
only look at: if I win

00:18:58.300 --> 00:18:58.700
what can I get

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:01.833
But of course

00:19:01.833 --> 00:19:04.400
it's not that all these fearless titans

00:19:04.600 --> 00:19:05.233
were simply born with nerves of steel

00:19:05.233 --> 00:19:07.233
A lot of the time

00:19:07.833 --> 00:19:10.100
what they rely on is endless drilling

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:11.200
An SAS instructor in the British
Special Air Service

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:14.233
once said something

00:19:14.466 --> 00:19:16.800
you could call the ultimate footnote
to the fearlessness dial

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:19.466
He said: "When it doesn't matter

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:21.066
train as if everything is at stake

00:19:21.066 --> 00:19:23.900
so that when it's life or death

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:26.633
you can fight as if nothing matters"

00:19:27.266 --> 00:19:30.200
This is my favorite line in the whole book

00:19:30.433 --> 00:19:31.900
Fear is the first lock
society puts on you

00:19:31.900 --> 00:19:33.233
and the next, the second lock

00:19:33.233 --> 00:19:34.800
is reputation

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:37.500
your reputation in others' eyes

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:38.600
You could also call this lock
the gaze of others

00:19:39.233 --> 00:19:40.133
hell is other people

00:19:40.133 --> 00:19:41.333
Society teaches you

00:19:41.333 --> 00:19:42.666
to guard your face

00:19:42.666 --> 00:19:44.833
to win social approval

00:19:44.833 --> 00:19:46.233
don't let people think
you don't fit in

00:19:46.233 --> 00:19:49.133
Of course, much of fitting in

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:50.700
also comes from our instinct
as social animals

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:53.000
And what this lock seals away

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:55.333
is your independent judgment
and confidence

00:19:55.666 --> 00:19:57.033
So how powerful is this lock?

00:19:57.033 --> 00:19:58.733
In the 1950s

00:19:58.733 --> 00:20:01.000
American social psychologist Solomon Asch

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:02.266
ran a classic psychology experiment

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:04.500
that captures it best

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:05.800
He brought nine people into a room

00:20:06.033 --> 00:20:07.266
and showed them slides

00:20:07.266 --> 00:20:08.533
On the left was a line

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:11.066
on the right, three lines

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:12.400
He asked which one matched
the left in length

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:13.833
The difficulty of this question

00:20:13.833 --> 00:20:15.300
is frankly an insult to your intelligence

00:20:15.733 --> 00:20:17.200
The answer is obvious at a glance

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:17.800
But the catch was

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:19.133
of the nine people

00:20:19.266 --> 00:20:20.666
eight were plants

00:20:20.666 --> 00:20:22.466
They deliberately, in unison

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:24.700
gave the same wrong answer

00:20:24.866 --> 00:20:25.400
Then it came to the ninth person

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:28.066
This person

00:20:28.333 --> 00:20:29.633
was the only true test subject

00:20:30.033 --> 00:20:32.133
So what would he choose?

00:20:32.133 --> 00:20:34.900
The result: 76% of people

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:36.266
went along with the others
at least once and got it wrong

00:20:36.266 --> 00:20:36.933
Asked afterward

00:20:36.933 --> 00:20:38.400
many of them said

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:40.466
I knew what the answer was

00:20:40.733 --> 00:20:43.233
but I didn't dare be different
from everyone else

00:20:43.233 --> 00:20:44.200
See, more than having
their intelligence insulted

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:46.500
what they cared about more was

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:48.033
not being the one
who's different from the group

00:20:48.033 --> 00:20:50.633
This pull of the group

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:52.266
may be one of the most powerful forces
in human society

00:20:52.633 --> 00:20:53.133
Most people can't withstand it

00:20:53.133 --> 00:20:55.700
But

00:20:56.066 --> 00:20:57.100
this experiment has a wonderful twist too

00:20:57.100 --> 00:20:59.066
The psychologist Asch

00:20:59.333 --> 00:21:00.100
later ran the experiment again

00:21:00.100 --> 00:21:02.333
And this time

00:21:02.466 --> 00:21:04.000
he planted one defector
among the eight

00:21:04.433 --> 00:21:06.600
having him give the correct answer

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:08.833
And just this one lone defector

00:21:09.266 --> 00:21:10.400
instantly shattered
the group's spell

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:12.000
That true subject

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:13.900
could now, every time,
follow the defector

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:15.666
and choose right. One defector

00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:17.100
was enough to break the whole illusion

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:18.200
But the problem is

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:19.933
in real life

00:21:20.333 --> 00:21:22.600
who's willing to be that defector?

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:24.333
Risking your boss's wrath,
your colleagues' rejection

00:21:24.433 --> 00:21:26.233
the ridicule of authority

00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:27.600
this takes enormous nerve

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:29.033
And there's a kind of person

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:30.933
who has exactly that nerve

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:34.033
the psychopath, of course

00:21:34.633 --> 00:21:36.900
because they simply don't care
what others think of them

00:21:37.333 --> 00:21:39.266
Here's a detail from the book
that stuck with me

00:21:39.266 --> 00:21:39.866
The first author, Dutton, told
the second author

00:21:39.866 --> 00:21:42.100
McNab

00:21:42.666 --> 00:21:44.733
about a fascinating game-theory experiment

00:21:45.133 --> 00:21:46.500
The setup of that experiment
goes roughly like this

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:47.533
There are 20 phone booths

00:21:47.533 --> 00:21:49.333
lined up in a row

00:21:49.666 --> 00:21:50.333
and you're locked in one of them

00:21:50.333 --> 00:21:51.800
In front of you

00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:53.333
is a big red button

00:21:53.466 --> 00:21:56.066
The rules go like this

00:21:56.266 --> 00:21:57.533
everyone stays in their booth
for 10 minutes

00:21:57.533 --> 00:21:59.933
If no one presses

00:22:00.233 --> 00:22:00.600
then everyone walks away with 10,000 pounds

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:03.133
But

00:22:03.266 --> 00:22:06.200
the moment one person presses early

00:22:06.433 --> 00:22:07.700
that person walks away with 2,500 pounds

00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:09.400
and the other 19

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:11.600
get nothing

00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:12.400
These booths are all in one room

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:14.133
and they're transparent

00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:16.433
everyone can see each other

00:22:16.433 --> 00:22:17.466
All right, the countdown begins

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:18.833
what would you do?

00:22:18.833 --> 00:22:20.533
McNab heard it out

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:22.000
and without a second thought
said "I'd press it"

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:23.733
Dutton was stunned

00:22:24.066 --> 00:22:25.866
and said "You wouldn't hesitate
even a second?"

00:22:25.866 --> 00:22:28.333
McNab looked utterly baffled

00:22:28.433 --> 00:22:29.600
looking at Dutton
as if he were an idiot

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:30.633
He said "Of course I'd press it

00:22:30.933 --> 00:22:31.833
why wouldn't I?"

00:22:31.833 --> 00:22:33.066
In this experiment

00:22:33.066 --> 00:22:35.000
most people's choice is

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:36.133
to at least wrestle with it for a while

00:22:36.133 --> 00:22:37.000
More or less

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:38.100
they've all thought about

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:38.733
waiting out the full 10 minutes

00:22:38.733 --> 00:22:40.066
so everyone together

00:22:40.466 --> 00:22:40.900
each gets 10,000

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:43.533
But

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:45.200
McNab's mind ran on a different track

00:22:45.200 --> 00:22:46.300
He said: among the 20 people

00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:47.333
some will panic

00:22:47.333 --> 00:22:48.500
some will be suspicious

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:49.800
some will have itchy fingers

00:22:49.866 --> 00:22:51.900
some are just plain selfish

00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:53.900
What makes you bet these 19 strangers

00:22:54.266 --> 00:22:55.466
will all hold out for 10 minutes?

00:22:55.466 --> 00:22:56.933
So since someone is going to press

00:22:57.200 --> 00:22:58.833
why shouldn't that person be me?

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:00.033
This is McNab's logic

00:23:00.033 --> 00:23:02.333
But really

00:23:02.333 --> 00:23:05.266
the true reason most people hesitate

00:23:05.266 --> 00:23:07.433
frankly isn't that they can't
do the math

00:23:07.633 --> 00:23:09.833
it's that they're afraid
of being the selfish one

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:10.900
You're afraid the other 19 will hate you

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:12.866
What you care about

00:23:13.200 --> 00:23:15.466
is really the gaze of others

00:23:15.466 --> 00:23:16.833
And McNab's answer is a cold

00:23:17.100 --> 00:23:18.333
purely rational calculation

00:23:18.333 --> 00:23:19.300
"The others might hate you

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:20.666
so what?

00:23:20.666 --> 00:23:21.866
You walked off with 2,500

00:23:22.266 --> 00:23:23.733
they got nothing"

00:23:23.733 --> 00:23:25.633
This is the psychopath's

00:23:25.933 --> 00:23:28.433
key to opening this second lock

00:23:28.633 --> 00:23:31.866
They simply don't care
how others judge you

00:23:32.133 --> 00:23:34.100
just bluntly walking
straight the other way

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:36.066
And this really does let psychopaths

00:23:36.533 --> 00:23:37.900
come out ahead much of the time

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:40.133
For example, research finds

00:23:40.133 --> 00:23:42.266
on the personality trait of agreeableness

00:23:42.266 --> 00:23:45.600
men who score above average

00:23:45.933 --> 00:23:46.700
earn about 18% less than average

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:48.300
Agreeableness

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:50.633
is whether you care a lot about

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:51.600
tending to and accommodating
others' emotions

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:52.333
Put plainly

00:23:52.333 --> 00:23:55.200
high agreeableness

00:23:55.333 --> 00:23:56.466
means you care more about
others' eyes and judgment

00:23:56.466 --> 00:23:58.000
And the more you care

00:23:58.466 --> 00:24:00.300
the less you earn

00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:02.666
The reason behind it is actually simple

00:24:02.666 --> 00:24:05.533
People who don't care much
what others think

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:07.100
are bolder about asking
for more at the table

00:24:07.100 --> 00:24:08.466
They're bolder about saying no

00:24:08.666 --> 00:24:11.066
bolder about saying I want this

00:24:11.066 --> 00:24:13.500
which in turn earns them more for themselves

00:24:14.033 --> 00:24:14.533
and even wins them more respect

00:24:14.533 --> 00:24:17.133
McNab

00:24:17.466 --> 00:24:19.600
told a story from his own experience

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:21.200
When he was starting a company

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:23.900
he rejected a job applicant

00:24:24.133 --> 00:24:25.866
And that applicant wrote back an email

00:24:25.933 --> 00:24:27.900
The gist of the email was this

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:31.100
He said: "After careful consideration

00:24:31.300 --> 00:24:33.833
I cannot accept your company's
decision to reject my employment

00:24:33.833 --> 00:24:35.033
This year I've been fortunate
to receive many rejections

00:24:35.033 --> 00:24:37.866
but with respect

00:24:38.133 --> 00:24:38.600
your company's rejection
doesn't meet my current needs

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:41.733
Therefore

00:24:42.333 --> 00:24:45.033
I'll be starting work at your company
in the second week of June"

00:24:45.066 --> 00:24:47.800
McNab and his partner read it
and cracked up laughing

00:24:48.066 --> 00:24:49.400
and then they really did give
this person a job

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:51.066
This applicant's manner

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:53.200
was extremely psychopathic

00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:54.666
as in: never mind what you think of me

00:24:55.000 --> 00:24:56.800
this job is mine

00:24:56.933 --> 00:24:59.133
And that's exactly how it should be

00:24:59.133 --> 00:25:00.400
But the group's grip on our decisions

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:02.500
doesn't only come from this

00:25:02.500 --> 00:25:03.733
caring about the gaze of others

00:25:03.733 --> 00:25:04.733
there's another

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:06.500
very important pathway

00:25:06.733 --> 00:25:09.600
and that's social comparison

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:10.500
We don't just focus on what we can get

00:25:10.500 --> 00:25:13.466
much of the time we

00:25:13.466 --> 00:25:14.633
focus more on whether what we got,
compared to others

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:16.533
is fair

00:25:16.533 --> 00:25:18.433
And for most people

00:25:18.433 --> 00:25:21.066
this fixation on fairness

00:25:21.400 --> 00:25:22.433
can even matter more
than pure material gain

00:25:22.433 --> 00:25:23.733
And on this point

00:25:23.733 --> 00:25:25.500
psychopaths' reaction

00:25:26.033 --> 00:25:27.466
is also utterly unlike ordinary people's

00:25:27.466 --> 00:25:28.533
For example, there's a game
called the

00:25:28.533 --> 00:25:29.900
ultimatum game

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:32.300
The rules

00:25:32.500 --> 00:25:33.900
are simple: two people split a sum of money

00:25:33.900 --> 00:25:35.400
A proposes the split

00:25:35.733 --> 00:25:36.600
B decides whether to accept

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:37.400
Say A says

00:25:37.400 --> 00:25:38.233
of this 100 yuan

00:25:38.233 --> 00:25:39.100
I take 70

00:25:39.100 --> 00:25:40.200
you take 30

00:25:40.433 --> 00:25:41.300
do you accept?

00:25:41.300 --> 00:25:42.500
If B accepts

00:25:42.500 --> 00:25:43.933
the money is split that way

00:25:43.933 --> 00:25:46.033
But if B refuses

00:25:46.533 --> 00:25:47.833
then neither person gets a cent

00:25:47.833 --> 00:25:49.700
A Japanese research team

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:51.033
had psychopaths and ordinary people

00:25:51.333 --> 00:25:52.133
each play this game

00:25:52.133 --> 00:25:55.033
And they found

00:25:55.333 --> 00:25:56.500
psychopaths accepted
more unfair offers

00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:57.533
A gives you only 20%

00:25:57.533 --> 00:25:58.466
fine, I'll take it

00:25:58.466 --> 00:26:00.266
gives you only 10%

00:26:00.633 --> 00:26:02.466
fine, it beats zero

00:26:02.466 --> 00:26:03.866
As a result, the money psychopaths earned

00:26:04.333 --> 00:26:05.533
far exceeded ordinary people's

00:26:05.533 --> 00:26:07.733
Because the ordinary person

00:26:07.733 --> 00:26:09.466
faced with an offer that's too unfair

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:10.866
will choose to burn it all down

00:26:10.866 --> 00:26:12.133
I'd rather take nothing

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:14.433
and drag you down with me

00:26:14.433 --> 00:26:16.100
But to a psychopath

00:26:16.100 --> 00:26:17.533
this money is exactly the same

00:26:17.866 --> 00:26:19.700
as money picked up off the ground

00:26:20.233 --> 00:26:21.800
And in fact it really is the same

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:24.733
In a psychopath's eyes

00:26:25.033 --> 00:26:26.900
fairness is probably
the most expensive human emotion

00:26:26.900 --> 00:26:28.333
It makes you give up
what's in your hand

00:26:28.333 --> 00:26:30.933
just to punish someone

00:26:31.333 --> 00:26:32.733
who may not even care
that you're punishing them

00:26:32.733 --> 00:26:36.900
Dutton draws a metaphor

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:38.600
He says: every time you hold a grudge,
resent, self-pity, sulk

00:26:38.733 --> 00:26:41.533
and feel the whole world
has wronged you

00:26:41.533 --> 00:26:42.700
you're handing the keys
to your emotions to others

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:45.000
letting them move into your heart

00:26:45.300 --> 00:26:47.333
and trash the place

00:26:47.333 --> 00:26:49.466
What a psychopath does instead

00:26:49.633 --> 00:26:51.400
is hang up a No Vacancy sign

00:26:51.700 --> 00:26:54.866
and never rent out their feelings

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:57.666
This is the psychopath's key
to the second lock

00:26:57.666 --> 00:26:59.400
Then let's look at the third lock

00:26:59.400 --> 00:27:00.200
that socialization adds to us

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:02.133
empathy

00:27:02.133 --> 00:27:05.200
Probably ever since you can remember

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:07.233
society has drilled one thing into you:
be kind

00:27:07.300 --> 00:27:09.266
be considerate, think of others

00:27:09.533 --> 00:27:10.900
be able to feel others' pain

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:12.066
Empathy is a virtue

00:27:12.100 --> 00:27:14.266
a good quality

00:27:14.866 --> 00:27:16.000
a mark of being humane

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:18.066
Your empathy dial

00:27:18.066 --> 00:27:20.066
gets turned all the way up like this

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:22.933
and finally welded in place there

00:27:22.933 --> 00:27:24.700
The book A Fatal Compassion
we covered before

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:27.533
describes an even more extreme case

00:27:27.533 --> 00:27:29.833
where even an entire civilization's empathy

00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:32.133
gets collectively welded at maximum

00:27:32.133 --> 00:27:34.533
so that today people barely even wonder

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:36.433
can this dial still be adjusted at all?

00:27:37.066 --> 00:27:38.100
Psychopaths can

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:39.400
For many years

00:27:39.400 --> 00:27:41.633
academia long believed

00:27:41.633 --> 00:27:43.200
the most universal trait psychopaths share

00:27:43.433 --> 00:27:44.133
is that they can't empathize

00:27:44.133 --> 00:27:46.800
that their brains

00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:47.900
lack the neural connections

00:27:48.233 --> 00:27:50.033
and biochemistry empathy requires

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:53.100
that they're born cold-blooded

00:27:53.400 --> 00:27:54.066
But this view has now
been overturned by experiment

00:27:54.066 --> 00:27:56.400
Researchers

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:57.800
took a batch of psychopaths
with criminal records

00:27:57.900 --> 00:27:59.466
and slid them into a brain scanner

00:27:59.466 --> 00:28:00.233
They played them a set of videos

00:28:00.233 --> 00:28:01.866
On screen

00:28:01.866 --> 00:28:04.300
two hands were interacting

00:28:04.500 --> 00:28:05.833
for example, one hand hurting the other

00:28:05.933 --> 00:28:06.500
The experiment had two rounds

00:28:06.500 --> 00:28:07.666
In the first round

00:28:07.666 --> 00:28:08.666
they gave no instructions

00:28:09.033 --> 00:28:09.866
just had them watch

00:28:09.866 --> 00:28:12.800
And they found

00:28:12.800 --> 00:28:14.066
the mirror neuron system
in the psychopaths' brains

00:28:14.533 --> 00:28:15.333
barely reacted at all

00:28:15.333 --> 00:28:17.900
Mirror neurons

00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:18.900
are a neural network dedicated to

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:21.300
replicating what others feel

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:23.633
one of the foundations of empathy

00:28:23.633 --> 00:28:26.033
Seeing one hand hurt another

00:28:26.266 --> 00:28:29.000
a normal brain automatically
winces in pain too

00:28:29.233 --> 00:28:30.100
But the psychopaths' brains
were completely unmoved

00:28:30.100 --> 00:28:31.666
Compared with ordinary people

00:28:31.833 --> 00:28:33.666
the gap was indeed huge

00:28:33.666 --> 00:28:34.200
But then came the second round

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:36.000
This time

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:37.666
before playing the video

00:28:37.833 --> 00:28:40.500
the researchers added
a simple instruction

00:28:40.500 --> 00:28:43.066
They told them: try to put yourself
in the other's place

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:44.433
and feel what they might be feeling

00:28:44.433 --> 00:28:46.100
Just that one sentence

00:28:46.433 --> 00:28:48.600
and everything turned upside down

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:49.033
The psychopaths' mirror neuron system

00:28:49.033 --> 00:28:52.200
suddenly

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:52.666
became just as active
as ordinary people's

00:28:52.666 --> 00:28:53.866
In other words

00:28:53.866 --> 00:28:55.100
they can empathize

00:28:55.266 --> 00:28:57.833
they always could

00:28:58.300 --> 00:28:58.933
they just don't switch
the feature on by default

00:28:58.933 --> 00:29:01.433
So

00:29:01.433 --> 00:29:02.466
when it comes to empathy,
psychopaths and ordinary people

00:29:02.466 --> 00:29:04.333
differ fundamentally in this:

00:29:04.733 --> 00:29:06.666
psychopaths kept the control

00:29:06.666 --> 00:29:07.500
It's as if they hold a remote

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:08.633
switch it on when they want

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:10.633
off when they want

00:29:10.633 --> 00:29:11.800
While many ordinary people's remote

00:29:11.800 --> 00:29:14.300
has been confiscated by socialization

00:29:14.300 --> 00:29:16.833
by cultural values

00:29:16.833 --> 00:29:18.033
So what happens when the empathy switch
is welded to on?

00:29:18.533 --> 00:29:20.400
There's an experiment
that illustrates it well

00:29:20.866 --> 00:29:22.333
In the late 1990s

00:29:22.333 --> 00:29:23.666
the US Marine Corps

00:29:23.666 --> 00:29:25.333
did something very interesting

00:29:25.666 --> 00:29:27.600
They went to Wall Street
to learn from the pros

00:29:27.833 --> 00:29:30.133
What could warriors learn
from stock traders?

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:31.466
Because they found

00:29:31.466 --> 00:29:34.500
the trading floor and the battlefield
have a lot in common

00:29:34.700 --> 00:29:36.300
both are information overload

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:38.333
both have situations
changing by the second

00:29:38.333 --> 00:29:40.500
both involve, amid enormous uncertainty

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:42.066
making high-risk decisions

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:43.800
Those Wall Street brokers

00:29:43.800 --> 00:29:45.833
face walls of data every day

00:29:45.833 --> 00:29:47.066
a floor full of bids

00:29:47.066 --> 00:29:50.400
deciding within seconds,
daring to bet hundreds of millions

00:29:50.666 --> 00:29:52.666
This decision speed and pressure

00:29:52.666 --> 00:29:55.100
is exactly what generals
need most on the battlefield

00:29:55.466 --> 00:29:56.666
Learning from them wasn't enough

00:29:56.666 --> 00:29:58.733
they also staged a simulated showdown

00:29:59.000 --> 00:30:01.900
They built two identical
simulated war rooms

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:03.900
fed in the same battlefield data

00:30:03.933 --> 00:30:05.333
put the generals in one room

00:30:05.333 --> 00:30:06.933
the brokers in the other

00:30:06.933 --> 00:30:08.733
and had them fight the same battle

00:30:08.933 --> 00:30:11.066
The result: both sides won

00:30:11.100 --> 00:30:12.666
but there was one key difference

00:30:12.900 --> 00:30:14.033
On the stock brokers' side

00:30:14.033 --> 00:30:15.833
more soldiers survived

00:30:15.833 --> 00:30:17.866
more warships and planes were saved

00:30:17.866 --> 00:30:20.000
casualties far below the generals' side

00:30:20.466 --> 00:30:21.266
Why?

00:30:21.433 --> 00:30:22.100
Analyzing it afterward

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:23.800
they found one huge difference

00:30:24.033 --> 00:30:26.233
Those brokers weren't fighting a war at all

00:30:26.233 --> 00:30:27.633
they were solving equations

00:30:27.900 --> 00:30:28.833
Data comes in

00:30:28.833 --> 00:30:29.700
runs through the math

00:30:29.700 --> 00:30:31.033
then outputs a decision

00:30:31.300 --> 00:30:33.033
There's no emotional component in it

00:30:33.033 --> 00:30:34.900
only pure probability calculation

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:37.833
In a very psychopathic way

00:30:37.833 --> 00:30:41.133
they made cold decisions
with empathy switched off

00:30:41.600 --> 00:30:42.866
And those generals

00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:45.000
the generals were reading data too

00:30:45.066 --> 00:30:47.133
but they'd been on a real battlefield

00:30:47.266 --> 00:30:49.866
They knew what a wrong decision means

00:30:50.266 --> 00:30:51.633
It means any decision you make

00:30:51.633 --> 00:30:54.000
could keep some soldiers
from ever going home

00:30:54.333 --> 00:30:56.000
So with their every decision

00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:57.900
a lot more crowds into their minds

00:30:58.333 --> 00:30:59.033
They'd think

00:30:59.033 --> 00:31:00.233
can we think it over again

00:31:00.233 --> 00:31:01.833
is there a safer option

00:31:01.833 --> 00:31:03.466
can we lose fewer men

00:31:03.866 --> 00:31:05.700
This kind of care for human life

00:31:05.700 --> 00:31:06.833
is completely understandable

00:31:06.833 --> 00:31:08.266
even admirable

00:31:08.633 --> 00:31:09.633
But the problem is

00:31:09.633 --> 00:31:11.666
just this tiny bit of extra hesitation

00:31:11.666 --> 00:31:13.633
this tiny bit of extra goodwill

00:31:13.633 --> 00:31:15.066
ate into reaction time

00:31:15.066 --> 00:31:17.033
missed the optimal window to act

00:31:17.333 --> 00:31:19.466
and in the end cost more lives

00:31:19.466 --> 00:31:21.433
more warships, more planes

00:31:22.066 --> 00:31:23.900
The price of empathy welded to on

00:31:23.900 --> 00:31:27.333
is that this goodwill itself
creates casualties

00:31:27.666 --> 00:31:29.666
Not that you botched it
because you're cold-blooded

00:31:29.666 --> 00:31:33.066
but precisely because you're too humane,
you botched it

00:31:33.466 --> 00:31:34.833
Pushing this question further

00:31:34.833 --> 00:31:37.800
there's actually a trade-off
between empathy on and off

00:31:38.400 --> 00:31:39.433
When empathy is strong

00:31:39.433 --> 00:31:40.233
people's attention

00:31:40.233 --> 00:31:42.433
tends to land on more concrete things

00:31:42.800 --> 00:31:44.400
like individual soldiers

00:31:44.400 --> 00:31:45.733
individual faces

00:31:46.200 --> 00:31:47.633
And when empathy is weak

00:31:47.633 --> 00:31:48.466
people's attention

00:31:48.466 --> 00:31:50.633
tends toward the grander

00:31:50.633 --> 00:31:52.000
more abstract level

00:31:52.333 --> 00:31:53.266
Different focus

00:31:53.266 --> 00:31:55.300
means a completely different
decision orientation

00:31:55.433 --> 00:31:56.900
Each has its own blind spots

00:31:56.933 --> 00:31:58.933
But at least in that experiment

00:31:58.933 --> 00:32:01.833
the weaker-empathy side
made the better decisions

00:32:02.533 --> 00:32:03.300
All right, at this point

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:05.233
we've covered all three locks

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:07.733
First, fear, locking up your decisiveness

00:32:07.900 --> 00:32:11.200
Second, caring too much about others' judgment,
locking up your independence

00:32:11.300 --> 00:32:13.400
Third, empathy welded to on

00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:14.933
locking up your composure

00:32:15.233 --> 00:32:17.533
Three locks, three tools of domestication

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:18.833
But our solution

00:32:18.833 --> 00:32:20.800
isn't to shut off empathy entirely

00:32:20.800 --> 00:32:22.200
throw away morality entirely

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:24.933
and become a totally cold decision machine

00:32:25.133 --> 00:32:27.400
What we want is to take back
the power to adjust

00:32:27.666 --> 00:32:29.600
not to turn the dial to max forever

00:32:29.600 --> 00:32:31.200
nor to min forever

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:33.433
but, like a good psychopath

00:32:33.433 --> 00:32:35.600
to once again have the right
to adjust it

00:32:36.300 --> 00:32:39.066
But here there's actually
a rather unsettling corollary

00:32:39.333 --> 00:32:41.666
Since you can turn ruthlessness,
fearlessness

00:32:41.666 --> 00:32:44.066
and low empathy up a notch

00:32:44.433 --> 00:32:47.800
then of course you can turn them up
two, three, ten notches

00:32:48.133 --> 00:32:50.066
So when exactly should you stop?

00:32:50.666 --> 00:32:52.133
Remember what we talked about earlier

00:32:52.133 --> 00:32:54.866
the matter of McNab
and that shepherd boy?

00:32:55.400 --> 00:32:57.333
In that Iraq operation, McNab

00:32:57.333 --> 00:32:58.733
let the boy go

00:32:58.800 --> 00:33:01.033
and sure enough
their position was exposed

00:33:01.033 --> 00:33:02.466
nearly wiping out the whole squad

00:33:02.833 --> 00:33:03.500
That choice

00:33:03.500 --> 00:33:06.200
made him the model of a good psychopath

00:33:06.200 --> 00:33:07.333
and left him with that line

00:33:07.333 --> 00:33:09.100
praised by many

00:33:09.100 --> 00:33:10.333
"We are the SAS

00:33:10.333 --> 00:33:11.866
we are not the SS"

00:33:12.266 --> 00:33:13.633
But one time Dutton

00:33:13.633 --> 00:33:16.333
pressed him with a very fierce question
to his face

00:33:16.700 --> 00:33:19.000
Staring into McNab's eyes, he asked

00:33:19.033 --> 00:33:20.700
he said: let's suppose now

00:33:20.700 --> 00:33:23.117
that you could have been 100% certain

00:33:23.117 --> 00:33:24.233
killing this boy

00:33:24.233 --> 00:33:25.600
would complete the mission

00:33:25.600 --> 00:33:27.400
and bring the whole squad home alive

00:33:27.533 --> 00:33:28.833
then how would you choose?

00:33:29.300 --> 00:33:32.233
McNab first tried to dodge
answering head-on

00:33:32.266 --> 00:33:33.200
He fudged it

00:33:33.200 --> 00:33:35.400
saying "there's no certainty
on the battlefield"

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:37.666
Then he told the truth

00:33:37.700 --> 00:33:40.833
He said "but if I really could be certain

00:33:40.866 --> 00:33:43.866
then that boy's fate
might have been different"

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:47.533
Dutton asked: how many notches
can that ruthlessness dial go up?

00:33:48.033 --> 00:33:50.033
And here's how Dutton
wrote it in the book

00:33:50.233 --> 00:33:52.600
He saw, behind McNab's
blue eyes

00:33:52.600 --> 00:33:54.233
the temperature plunge

00:33:54.400 --> 00:33:55.233
He shrugged

00:33:55.233 --> 00:33:57.133
and said "as high as you like"

00:33:57.866 --> 00:34:00.666
Between a good psychopath
and a bad one

00:34:00.666 --> 00:34:03.533
sometimes there's only one notch
on the dial

00:34:04.033 --> 00:34:06.000
If you always walk beside the abyss

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:06.633
then really

00:34:06.633 --> 00:34:09.233
nothing can guarantee
you won't fall in

00:34:10.033 --> 00:34:12.466
All right, that's our reading of
The Good Psychopath's Guide to Success

00:34:12.466 --> 00:34:13.833
for you, right here

